And now you get to hear from the creator himself.

9
00:01:04,959 --> 00:01:05,099
Okay.

10
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:06,920
Should we pop that on?

11
00:01:07,740 --> 00:01:07,860
Yeah.

12
00:01:10,460 --> 00:01:11,539
Okay, thanks.

13
00:01:11,780 --> 00:01:12,300
Thank you, Gil.

14
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:13,680
Thank you for inviting me here.

15
00:01:14,440 --> 00:01:15,880
I hope you're enjoying the course.

16
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:23,640
Today, I want to tell you a little about why there are so many matrices that are low rank in the world.

17
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:27,240
So, as computational mathematicians, Gil and myself,

18
00:01:27,380 --> 00:01:28,140
we come across matrices.

19
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:30,179
Low rank matrices all the time.

20
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:35,340
And we started wondering as a community, why?

21
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:40,940
What is it about the matrices, the problems that we're looking at?

22
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:43,620
What makes low rank matrices appear?

23
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:48,160
And today, I want to kind of give you that story, or at least an overview of that story.

24
00:01:49,420 --> 00:01:55,539
So, for this class, X is going to be an n by n real matrix.

25
00:01:57,380 --> 00:01:58,200
So, nice and simple.

26
00:01:58,300 --> 00:01:58,660
Nice and square.

27
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:04,720
And you already know, or are very comfortable with, the singular values of a matrix.

28
00:02:05,400 --> 00:02:11,039
So, the singular values of a matrix, as you know, are a sequence of numbers

29
00:02:11,039 --> 00:02:16,220
that are monotonically non-increasing

30
00:02:16,220 --> 00:02:22,240
that tell us all kinds of things about the matrix X.

31
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:28,180
For example, the number of...

32
00:02:28,180 --> 00:02:33,260
The number of non-zero singular values tell us the rank of the matrix X.

33
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:41,800
And they also, you probably know, tell us how well a matrix X can be approximated by a low rank matrix.

34
00:02:42,720 --> 00:02:46,800
So, let me just write two facts down that you already are familiar with.

35
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:49,560
So, here's a fact.

36
00:02:51,060 --> 00:02:57,220
That if I look at the number of non-zero singular values in X,

37
00:02:57,220 --> 00:03:01,840
so, I'm imagining there's going to be K non-zero singular values,

38
00:03:02,260 --> 00:03:08,740
then we can say a few things about X.

39
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:14,380
For example, X, the rank of X, as we know, is K.

40
00:03:16,040 --> 00:03:18,440
The number of non-zero singular values.

41
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:22,820
But we also know, from the SVD,

42
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:26,440
that we can decompose X into a sum of...

43
00:03:26,440 --> 00:03:27,160
into a sum of...

44
00:03:27,160 --> 00:03:28,579
of rank one matrices.

45
00:03:29,460 --> 00:03:31,720
In fact, a sum of K of them.

46
00:03:33,160 --> 00:03:35,940
So, because X is rank K,

47
00:03:36,359 --> 00:03:41,000
we can write down a low rank representation for X,

48
00:03:41,220 --> 00:03:43,100
and it involves K terms,

49
00:03:43,720 --> 00:03:45,800
like this.

50
00:03:47,340 --> 00:03:51,620
Each one of these vectors here is a column vector.

51
00:03:52,359 --> 00:03:54,000
So, if I draw this pictorially,

52
00:03:55,120 --> 00:03:57,140
this is a column vector.

53
00:03:57,260 --> 00:03:58,859
This guy looks like this, right?

54
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:00,460
And we have K of them.

55
00:04:01,359 --> 00:04:03,460
So, because X is rank K,

56
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:07,960
we can write X as a sum of K rank one matrices.

57
00:04:08,660 --> 00:04:11,660
And we also have an initial fact that we already know,

58
00:04:12,060 --> 00:04:17,860
that the dimension of the column space of X is equal to K.

59
00:04:18,060 --> 00:04:19,860
And the same with the row space.

60
00:04:20,860 --> 00:04:27,060
So, the col space of X equals the row space of X,

61
00:04:27,160 --> 00:04:28,240
the dimension.

62
00:04:31,280 --> 00:04:33,500
And that they all equal K.

63
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:37,600
So, there are three facts we can determine

64
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:41,200
from looking at this sequence of singular values

65
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:42,820
of a matrix X.

66
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:46,280
Of course, the singular value sequence is unique.

67
00:04:47,560 --> 00:04:49,280
X defines that singular...

68
00:04:49,280 --> 00:04:50,880
its own singular values.

69
00:04:51,960 --> 00:04:52,680
Okay.

70
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:55,880
What we're interested in here is

71
00:04:55,880 --> 00:04:56,160
what makes...

72
00:04:56,160 --> 00:04:57,160
what makes...

73
00:04:57,260 --> 00:04:58,160
X...

74
00:04:58,160 --> 00:04:59,840
what are the properties of X

75
00:04:59,840 --> 00:05:02,400
that make sure that the singular values

76
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:04,240
have a lot of zeros in that sequence?

77
00:05:05,220 --> 00:05:08,240
Can we try to understand

78
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:09,960
what kind of X makes that happen?

79
00:05:12,720 --> 00:05:14,820
And we really like matrices

80
00:05:14,820 --> 00:05:16,800
that have a lot of zeros here

81
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:18,500
for the following reason.

82
00:05:19,940 --> 00:05:21,160
If X is...

83
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:23,820
we say X is low rank,

84
00:05:28,160 --> 00:05:29,760
if the following holds, right?

85
00:05:29,760 --> 00:05:31,160
Because we...

86
00:05:31,160 --> 00:05:33,160
if we wanted to send X to our friend,

87
00:05:33,160 --> 00:05:35,160
we're imagining X is a picture

88
00:05:35,160 --> 00:05:39,160
where each entry is a pixel of that image.

89
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:43,160
If that matrix, that image, was low rank,

90
00:05:43,160 --> 00:05:48,160
we could send the picture to our friend in two ways.

91
00:05:48,160 --> 00:05:52,160
We could send one every single entry of X.

92
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:56,160
And for us to do that, we would have to send N squared.

93
00:05:56,160 --> 00:05:58,160
Pieces of information,

94
00:05:58,160 --> 00:06:00,160
because we'd have to send every entry.

95
00:06:00,160 --> 00:06:03,160
But if X is sufficiently low rank,

96
00:06:03,160 --> 00:06:07,160
we could also send then, our friend, the vectors.

97
00:06:07,160 --> 00:06:11,160
U, U1, V1, UK up to VK.

98
00:06:11,160 --> 00:06:13,160
And how many...

99
00:06:13,160 --> 00:06:16,160
how much pieces of data would we have to send our friend

100
00:06:16,160 --> 00:06:20,160
to get X to them if we sent in the low rank form?

101
00:06:20,160 --> 00:06:22,160
Well, here there's two...

102
00:06:22,160 --> 00:06:25,160
there's, um, 2N here, 2Y here,

103
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:27,160
2N here numbers.

104
00:06:27,160 --> 00:06:29,160
There's K of them.

105
00:06:29,160 --> 00:06:33,160
So we'd have to send 2KN numbers.

106
00:06:33,160 --> 00:06:37,160
And we strictly say a matrix is low rank

107
00:06:37,160 --> 00:06:41,160
if it's more efficient to send X to our friend

108
00:06:41,160 --> 00:06:45,160
in low rank form than in full rank form.

109
00:06:45,160 --> 00:06:46,160
Okay?

110
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:49,160
So this, of course, by a little calculation,

111
00:06:49,160 --> 00:06:52,160
just shows us that,

112
00:06:52,160 --> 00:06:55,160
provided the rank is less than half the size

113
00:06:55,160 --> 00:06:57,160
of the matrix,

114
00:06:57,160 --> 00:07:00,160
we are calling the matrix low rank.

115
00:07:00,160 --> 00:07:05,160
Now, often in practice,

116
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:10,160
we demand more...

117
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:14,160
we demand that K is much smaller

118
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:16,160
than this number,

119
00:07:16,160 --> 00:07:18,160
so that it's far more efficient

120
00:07:18,160 --> 00:07:21,160
to send our friend the matrix X in low rank form

121
00:07:21,160 --> 00:07:23,160
than in full rank form.

122
00:07:23,160 --> 00:07:24,160
Okay?

123
00:07:24,160 --> 00:07:27,160
So the colloquial use of the word low rank

124
00:07:27,160 --> 00:07:30,160
is, uh, is kind of this situation.

125
00:07:30,160 --> 00:07:33,160
But this is the strict definition of it.

126
00:07:33,160 --> 00:07:34,160
Okay.

127
00:07:34,160 --> 00:07:39,160
So what do, uh, low rank matrices look like?

128
00:07:39,160 --> 00:07:43,160
And to do that, I have, uh, some pictures for you.

