00:14:21,440 --> 00:14:23,280
which is me dividing this number

271
00:14:23,280 --> 00:14:24,880
by this number approximately.

272
00:14:27,340 --> 00:14:29,160
So triangular patterns

273
00:14:29,160 --> 00:14:31,080
are extremely bad for low rank.

274
00:14:32,100 --> 00:14:33,280
We need things,

275
00:14:33,460 --> 00:14:35,100
or we at least intuitively think

276
00:14:35,100 --> 00:14:35,940
that we need things

277
00:14:35,940 --> 00:14:38,180
aligned with the rows and columns.

278
00:14:38,960 --> 00:14:40,440
But the circle case

279
00:14:40,440 --> 00:14:43,260
happens to also be low rank.

280
00:14:45,760 --> 00:14:48,280
And so what happened

281
00:14:48,280 --> 00:14:49,460
to the Japanese flag?

282
00:14:54,160 --> 00:14:56,540
Why is the Japanese flag

283
00:14:56,540 --> 00:14:59,000
convenient for low rank?

284
00:14:59,980 --> 00:15:03,140
Well, it's the fact that it's a circle

285
00:15:03,140 --> 00:15:05,500
and there's lots of symmetry in a circle.

286
00:15:05,940 --> 00:15:07,760
So if I try to look at the rank

287
00:15:07,760 --> 00:15:08,940
of a circle,

288
00:15:09,760 --> 00:15:11,680
the Japanese flag,

289
00:15:12,160 --> 00:15:17,440
then I can bound this rank

290
00:15:17,440 --> 00:15:21,280
by decomposing the Japanese flag

291
00:15:21,280 --> 00:15:23,240
into two things.

292
00:15:23,980 --> 00:15:25,000
So this is going to be

293
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:26,540
less than or equal to

294
00:15:26,540 --> 00:15:30,300
the rank of a sum of two matrices.

295
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:32,120
And I'll do it

296
00:15:32,120 --> 00:15:33,760
so that the decomposition works out.

297
00:15:33,760 --> 00:15:35,020
I have the circle.

298
00:15:35,020 --> 00:15:36,520
I'm going to cut out

299
00:15:36,520 --> 00:15:38,860
a rank one piece

300
00:15:38,860 --> 00:15:41,460
that lives in the middle of this circle.

301
00:15:47,319 --> 00:15:47,840
Okay.

302
00:15:47,960 --> 00:15:49,260
And I'm going to cut out a square

303
00:15:49,260 --> 00:15:54,120
from the interior of that circle.

304
00:15:54,840 --> 00:15:54,939
Okay.

305
00:15:55,480 --> 00:15:57,000
And I can figure out, of course,

306
00:15:57,040 --> 00:15:58,100
the rank is just bounded

307
00:15:58,100 --> 00:15:59,500
by the sum of those two ranks.

308
00:16:00,500 --> 00:16:02,960
This guy is bounded by rank one

309
00:16:02,960 --> 00:16:04,800
because it's highly aligned

310
00:16:04,800 --> 00:16:05,420
with the grid.

311
00:16:06,680 --> 00:16:06,839
Okay.

312
00:16:07,120 --> 00:16:08,359
This is dark.

313
00:16:09,319 --> 00:16:11,199
So this guy is bounded by rank one.

314
00:16:11,979 --> 00:16:18,579
So this thing here

315
00:16:18,579 --> 00:16:23,439
plus one.

316
00:16:25,959 --> 00:16:29,280
And now I have to try to understand

317
00:16:29,280 --> 00:16:31,459
the rank of this piece.

318
00:16:32,740 --> 00:16:34,680
Now, this piece has lots of similarities.

319
00:16:34,800 --> 00:16:36,760
This is the matrix of symmetry.

320
00:16:36,760 --> 00:16:39,880
For example, we know that the rank of that matrix

321
00:16:39,880 --> 00:16:42,939
is the dimension of the column space

322
00:16:42,939 --> 00:16:45,680
and the dimension of the row space.

323
00:16:45,680 --> 00:16:48,099
So when we look at this matrix,

324
00:16:48,099 --> 00:16:49,540
because of symmetry,

325
00:16:49,540 --> 00:16:54,920
if I divide this matrix in half along the columns,

326
00:16:54,920 --> 00:16:58,199
all the columns on the left appear on the right.

327
00:16:58,199 --> 00:17:00,540
So the columns, for example,

328
00:17:00,540 --> 00:17:02,760
the rank of this matrix is the same as

329
00:17:02,760 --> 00:17:04,760
the rank of that matrix,

330
00:17:04,760 --> 00:17:06,640
because I didn't change the column space.

331
00:17:06,640 --> 00:17:08,079
Okay.

332
00:17:08,200 --> 00:17:11,359
Now I go again and divide along the rows.

333
00:17:13,560 --> 00:17:17,160
And now the row dimension of this matrix

334
00:17:17,160 --> 00:17:19,379
is the same as the top half,

335
00:17:19,500 --> 00:17:20,900
because as I wipe out those,

336
00:17:20,960 --> 00:17:23,099
I didn't change the dimension of the row space,

337
00:17:23,260 --> 00:17:25,060
because the rows are the same top-bottom.

338
00:17:26,220 --> 00:17:27,680
And so this becomes the rank

339
00:17:27,680 --> 00:17:29,900
of that tiny little matrix there.

340
00:17:31,020 --> 00:17:32,040
And because it's small,

341
00:17:32,580 --> 00:17:34,640
it won't have too large a rank.

342
00:17:34,760 --> 00:17:39,760
So this is definitely less than if I divide that up.

343
00:17:43,560 --> 00:17:45,960
Little guy here, it looks like that.

344
00:17:47,520 --> 00:17:52,520
Plus the other guy, it looks like that.

345
00:17:58,420 --> 00:17:59,260
Plus one.

346
00:18:00,520 --> 00:18:03,320
And so of course, the rows, the column,

347
00:18:03,320 --> 00:18:04,280
the rows are the same.

348
00:18:04,280 --> 00:18:04,720
Okay.

349
00:18:04,720 --> 00:18:06,160
So the row space of this matrix

350
00:18:06,160 --> 00:18:08,200
cannot be very high,

351
00:18:08,260 --> 00:18:09,920
because this is a very thin matrix.

352
00:18:10,400 --> 00:18:12,060
There's lots of zeros in that matrix,

353
00:18:12,180 --> 00:18:13,160
only a few ones.

354
00:18:13,680 --> 00:18:16,020
And so you can go along and do a bit of trig

355
00:18:16,020 --> 00:18:17,700
to try to figure out

356
00:18:17,700 --> 00:18:21,760
how many rows are non-zero in this matrix.

357
00:18:22,440 --> 00:18:25,360
And a bit of trig tells you,

358
00:18:25,760 --> 00:18:29,200
well, it depends on the radius of this original circle.

359
00:18:29,560 --> 00:18:31,560
So if I make the original radius R

360
00:18:31,560 --> 00:18:33,540
of this Japanese flag,

361
00:18:33,540 --> 00:18:36,540
then the bound that you end up getting

362
00:18:36,540 --> 00:18:39,000
will be for this matrix,

363
00:18:39,460 --> 00:18:43,320
R one minus square root two over two

364
00:18:43,320 --> 00:18:45,300
for this guy, a bit of trig.

365
00:18:46,240 --> 00:18:47,460
Better make sure that's an integer.

366
00:18:48,159 --> 00:18:49,740
And then again, here it's the same,

367
00:18:50,040 --> 00:18:51,159
but for the column space.

368
00:18:51,960 --> 00:18:53,460
So this is me just doing trig.

369
00:18:56,060 --> 00:18:56,540
Okay.

370
00:18:56,639 --> 00:18:57,899
And that's a bound on the rank.

371
00:18:57,980 --> 00:18:59,280
It happens to be extremely good.

372
00:18:59,680 --> 00:19:02,500
And if you work out what that rank is

373
00:19:02,500 --> 00:19:03,420
and try to look back,

374
00:19:03,659 --> 00:19:05,120
you'll find it's extremely efficient

375
00:19:05,120 --> 00:19:07,940
to send the Japanese flag to your friend

376
00:19:07,940 --> 00:19:09,460
in low rank form.

377
00:19:10,560 --> 00:19:11,800
Because it's not full rank

378
00:19:11,800 --> 00:19:13,300
because these numbers are so small.

379
00:19:13,780 --> 00:19:15,560
So this comes out to be

380
00:19:15,560 --> 00:19:19,540
like approximately a half R plus one.

381
00:19:20,840 --> 00:19:23,120
So much smaller than what you would expect.

382
00:19:23,720 --> 00:19:25,560
Because remember, a circle is almost

383
00:19:25,560 --> 00:19:28,480
the anti version of aligned with the grid.

384
00:19:29,180 --> 00:19:30,540
But yeah, it's still low rank.

385
00:19:32,659 --> 00:19:33,139
Okay.

386
00:19:33,540 --> 00:19:39,720
So, now, most matrices that we come up with

387
00:19:39,720 --> 00:19:40,940
in computational math

388
00:19:40,940 --> 00:19:43,620
are not exactly of finite rank.

389
00:19:44,139 --> 00:19:46,700
They are of numerical rank.

390
00:19:48,360 --> 00:19:50,440
And so I'll just define that.

391
00:19:51,460 --> 00:19:57,520
So, the numerical rank of a matrix

392
00:19:57,520 --> 00:19:59,360
is very similar to the rank,

393
00:19:59,440 --> 00:20:00,820
except we allow ourselves

394
00:20:00,820 --> 00:20:02,480
a little bit of wiggle room

395
00:20:02,480 --> 00:20:03,520
when we define it.

396
00:20:03,540 --> 00:20:03,580
Okay.

397
00:20:04,120 --> 00:20:05,860
And so that amount of wiggle room

398
00:20:05,860 --> 00:20:09,980
will be a parameter called toll,

399
00:20:10,620 --> 00:20:11,440
called epsilon.

400
00:20:11,940 --> 00:20:12,159
Okay.

401
00:20:12,200 --> 00:20:13,000
That's a tolerance.

402
00:20:13,100 --> 00:20:15,040
I'm thinking of epsilon as a tolerance.

403
00:20:16,540 --> 00:20:18,120
That's the amount of wiggle room

404
00:20:18,120 --> 00:20:19,060
I'm going to give myself.

405
00:20:20,860 --> 00:20:21,500
Okay.

406
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:25,440
And we say that the numerical rank,

407
00:20:26,920 --> 00:20:28,659
I'll put an epsilon there

408
00:20:28,659 --> 00:20:30,080
to denote numerical rank,

409
00:20:30,080 --> 00:20:31,960
is k.

410
00:20:32,700 --> 00:20:33,460
If,

411
00:20:33,540 --> 00:20:36,240
k is the first singular value,

412
00:20:36,560 --> 00:20:38,520
or the last singular value above epsilon,

413
00:20:38,980 --> 00:20:40,159
in the following sense,

414
00:20:40,240 --> 00:20:42,000
I'm copying the definition above,

415
00:20:42,379 --> 00:20:44,060
but with epsilons instead of zeros,

416
00:20:44,780 --> 00:20:46,379
if this singular value

417
00:20:46,379 --> 00:20:48,879
is less than epsilon,

418
00:20:49,320 --> 00:20:51,980
relatively,

419
00:20:52,560 --> 00:20:55,920
and the kth one was not below.

420
00:20:56,440 --> 00:20:59,520
So, k plus one is the first singular value

421
00:20:59,520 --> 00:21:02,460
below epsilon in this relative sense.

422
00:21:02,659 --> 00:21:03,420
So,

423
00:21:03,420 --> 00:21:04,560
so, of course,

424
00:21:05,640 --> 00:21:09,840
the rank of zero, x,

425
00:21:09,840 --> 00:21:10,800
if that was defined,

426
00:21:10,800 --> 00:21:13,060
is the same as the rank of x.

427
00:21:13,060 --> 00:21:14,700
Okay.

428
00:21:14,700 --> 00:21:16,960
So this is just allowing ourselves some wiggle room.

429
00:21:16,960 --> 00:21:20,960
But this is actually what we're interested more in in practice.

430
00:21:20,960 --> 00:21:21,840
All right.

431
00:21:21,840 --> 00:21:24,540
I don't want to necessarily send my friend the flag

432
00:21:24,540 --> 00:21:26,080
to exact precision.

433
00:21:26,080 --> 00:21:28,960
I would actually be happy to send my friend the flag

434
00:21:28,960 --> 00:21:32,320
up to 16 digits of precision, for example.

435
00:21:32,320 --> 00:21:35,200
They're not going to tell the difference between those two flags.

436
00:21:35,760 --> 00:21:40,220
And if I can get away with compressing the matrix a lot more

437
00:21:40,220 --> 00:21:43,360
once I have a little bit of wiggle room, that would be a good thing.

438
00:21:47,360 --> 00:21:53,460
So we know from the ERCAC in Young

439
00:21:53,460 --> 00:22:03,000
that the singular values tell us how well we can approximate x

440
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:04,980
by a low-rank matrix.

441
00:22:05,580 --> 00:22:12,480
In particular, we know that the k plus 1 singular value of x

442
00:22:12,480 --> 00:22:19,000
tells us how well x can be approximated by a rank k matrix.

443
00:22:20,120 --> 00:22:23,280
For example, when the rank was exactly k,

444
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,820
the sigma k plus 1 was 0.

445
00:22:27,740 --> 00:22:29,580
And then this came out to be 0,

446
00:22:29,580 --> 00:22:32,740
and we found that x was exactly a rank k matrix.

447
00:22:33,160 --> 00:22:35,800
Here, because we have the wiggle room, the epsilon,

448
00:22:36,120 --> 00:22:38,440
we get an approximation, not an exact.

449
00:22:39,180 --> 00:22:44,860
So this is telling us how well we can approximate x

450
00:22:44,860 --> 00:22:47,540
by a rank k matrix.

451
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:53,140
That's what the singular values are telling us.

452
00:22:53,460 --> 00:22:59,340
And so this allows us to try our best to compress matrices,

453
00:22:59,640 --> 00:23:05,560
but use low-rank approximation rather than doing things exactly.

454
00:23:07,520 --> 00:23:11,760
And of course, on a computer, when we're using floating-point arithmetic,

455
00:23:12,680 --> 00:23:15,980
or on a computer because we always round numbers

456
00:23:15,980 --> 00:23:18,940
to the nearest 16-digit number,

457
00:23:18,940 --> 00:23:21,360
if epsilon was 16 digits,

458
00:23:21,580 --> 00:23:23,400
your computer wouldn't be able to tell

459
00:23:23,400 --> 00:23:23,440
that x is a rank k matrix.

460
00:23:23,440 --> 00:23:27,720
So we can't tell the difference between x or x,

461
00:23:28,380 --> 00:23:30,140
the rank k approximation,

462
00:23:30,980 --> 00:23:34,360
if this number was satisfied this expression.

463
00:23:34,980 --> 00:23:38,160
Your computer would think of x and xk as the same matrix

464
00:23:38,160 --> 00:23:43,260
because it would inevitably round both to epsilon,

465
00:23:43,800 --> 00:23:44,480
within epsilon.

466
00:23:45,700 --> 00:23:49,540
So what kind of matrices are numerically of low rank?

467
00:23:53,440 --> 00:24:08,080
Of course, all low-rank matrices are numerically of low rank,

468
00:24:08,080 --> 00:24:10,920
because the wiggle room can only help you.

469
00:24:15,920 --> 00:24:17,620
But it's far more than that.

470
00:24:18,720 --> 00:24:20,980
There are many full-rank matrices,

471
00:24:20,980 --> 00:24:23,400
matrices that don't have any singular values,

472
00:24:23,400 --> 00:24:24,660
that are zero,

473
00:24:24,660 --> 00:24:27,880
but the singular values decay rapidly to zero,

474
00:24:27,880 --> 00:24:32,080
that are full-rank matrices with low numerical rank

475
00:24:32,080 --> 00:24:33,820
because of the wiggle room.

476
00:24:33,820 --> 00:24:38,820
So for example, here is the classic matrix

477
00:24:38,960 --> 00:24:41,520
that fits this regime.

478
00:24:43,160 --> 00:24:46,100
If I give you this, this is called the Hilbert matrix.

479
00:24:51,140 --> 00:24:53,240
This is a matrix that happens to have,

480
00:24:53,240 --> 00:24:56,140
extremely low numerical rank,

481
00:24:57,140 --> 00:24:58,540
but is actually full-rank.

482
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:03,820
Which means that I can approximate h to,

483
00:25:04,240 --> 00:25:07,180
by a rank k matrix where k is quite small,

484
00:25:07,740 --> 00:25:10,300
very well, provided you give me some wiggle room.

485
00:25:10,920 --> 00:25:13,820
But it's not a low-rank matrix in the sense

486
00:25:13,820 --> 00:25:15,460
that if epsilon was zero here,

487
00:25:15,700 --> 00:25:16,920
you didn't allow me the wiggle room,

488
00:25:17,240 --> 00:25:19,520
all the singular values of this matrix are positive.

489
00:25:20,580 --> 00:25:22,800
So it's of low rank,

490
00:25:22,800 --> 00:25:24,700
it's of low numerical rank,

491
00:25:24,700 --> 00:25:29,040
but not, it's not a low-rank matrix.

492
00:25:29,040 --> 00:25:32,460
The other classical case, classic example,

493
00:25:32,460 --> 00:25:35,520
which motivated a lot of the research in this area

494
00:25:35,520 --> 00:25:37,800
was the Vandermonde matrix.

495
00:25:37,800 --> 00:25:39,919
So here is the Vandermonde matrix.

496
00:25:48,560 --> 00:25:50,760
An n by n version of it.

497
00:25:50,760 --> 00:25:52,419
Think of the x i's as real.

498
00:25:52,800 --> 00:25:57,159
This is Vandermonde.

499
00:26:02,159 --> 00:26:04,419
This is the matrix that comes up when you try to do

500
00:26:04,419 --> 00:26:08,560
polynomial interpolation at real points.

501
00:26:08,560 --> 00:26:12,780
This is an extremely bad matrix to deal with

502
00:26:12,780 --> 00:26:16,040
because it's numerically low-rank.

503
00:26:16,040 --> 00:26:18,240
And often you actually want to solve

504
00:26:18,240 --> 00:26:21,139
a linear system with this matrix.

505
00:26:21,139 --> 00:26:22,500
And numerical low-rank,

506
00:26:22,500 --> 00:26:25,940
it implies that it's extremely hard to invert.

507
00:26:25,940 --> 00:26:29,720
So this is low-rank,

508
00:26:29,720 --> 00:26:31,860
numerical low-rank is not always good for you.

509
00:26:31,860 --> 00:26:32,300
Okay?

510
00:26:32,300 --> 00:26:40,580
Often we want the inverse,

511
00:26:40,580 --> 00:26:42,340
which exists.

512
00:26:42,340 --> 00:26:44,500
But it's difficult because V is low.

513
00:26:44,500 --> 00:26:45,040
It's very basic.

514
00:26:45,040 --> 00:26:48,900
It's very many times more complicated,

515
00:26:48,900 --> 00:26:51,240
because you do the binary formula

516
00:26:51,240 --> 00:26:51,940
and then your qua-etin,

517
00:26:51,940 --> 00:26:52,240
this chiarium, which is your q is less than 1,

518
00:26:52,240 --> 00:26:52,420
gives you a solve.

519
00:26:52,420 --> 00:26:52,500
What you would actually need to do is to,

520
00:26:52,500 --> 00:26:56,060
B has low numerical rank.

521
00:27:03,180 --> 00:27:03,980
Okay.

522
00:27:04,360 --> 00:27:06,280
So people have been trying to understand

523
00:27:06,280 --> 00:27:10,220
why these matrices are numerically of low rank

524
00:27:10,220 --> 00:27:11,540
for a number of years.

525
00:27:11,800 --> 00:27:15,140
And the classic reason

526
00:27:15,140 --> 00:27:17,800
why there are so many low rank matrices

527
00:27:17,800 --> 00:27:21,760
is because the world is smooth, as people say.

528
00:27:21,760 --> 00:27:23,879
They say the world is smooth.

529
00:27:25,240 --> 00:27:31,220
That's why matrices are of numerical low rank.

530
00:27:32,540 --> 00:27:34,480
And to illustrate that point,

531
00:27:35,520 --> 00:27:37,660
I will do an example.

532
00:27:39,100 --> 00:27:40,660
So this is classically understood

533
00:27:40,660 --> 00:27:44,760
by a man called Reed in 1983.

534
00:27:47,840 --> 00:27:51,320
And this is what his reason was.

535
00:27:51,320 --> 00:27:53,020
I have a picture of John Reed.

536
00:27:53,820 --> 00:27:54,960
He's not very famous,

537
00:27:55,139 --> 00:27:58,720
so I try to make sure his picture gets around.

538
00:28:00,320 --> 00:28:01,399
He's playing the piano.

539
00:28:01,560 --> 00:28:03,300
It's one of the only pictures I could find of him.

540
00:28:04,520 --> 00:28:06,520
So what is in this reason?

541
00:28:06,899 --> 00:28:07,879
Why do people say this?

542
00:28:08,480 --> 00:28:12,340
Well, here's an example that illustrates it.

543
00:28:12,700 --> 00:28:16,300
If I take a polynomial in two variables,

544
00:28:17,200 --> 00:28:18,320
and I...

